## Out-of-scope / Unrelated Findings (opt-out)

When work surfaces something unrelated to the current task, **by default**
auto-file a GitHub issue and link it in the PR's "Refactoring opportunities":
`gh issue create --title "<summary>" --body "<context>"
--label "out-of-scope,from-claude"`. **Opt out** by setting
`DISABLE_OUT_OF_SCOPE_ISSUES=true` as a repo variable; with opt-out, surface
the finding only in the PR description.

## Alternative Operating Mode: Single Feature Branch / Single PR

The default is the canonical 8-PR sequence in `repo-standards` PROMPT.md.
For focused work that would otherwise produce ≤3 PRs, an alternative is
sanctioned: one long-lived feature branch + one PR opened **early** (after
the first commit, draft); subscribe to PR activity (`gh pr subscribe <PR#>`
or the MCP equivalent) so CI/comment events arrive in-session; each commit
pushes to the same branch and re-triggers integrity workflows; mark
ready-for-review only at completion. Per-change ritual (plain-English plan →
edit → self-check → summary) still applies to every commit.

## Plan Management & Clean State Rule

**Plan Management & Clean State Rule (critical safety net).** When the
user asks you to create, update, or refresh a plan (or when you enter
plan mode multiple times in a long session):

* Always start a completely fresh plan file, OR
* Actively delete / remove / mark as completed all sections that have
  already been shipped and merged.
* Never append new sections to an old plan file that already contains
  finished work.
* Keep the plan file concise and focused only on remaining work.

This prevents plan bloat and redundant entries in long Claude sessions.

## Post-task self-check

After every turn that changes code, scan for things that should be codified
in `README.md`, this file, `.github/workflows/*.yml`, or `.github/dependabot.yml`
— new env vars, npm scripts, path filters, deploy targets, secrets, setup
steps, dependencies, conventions. Per `PROMPT.md` rule 2, **always include a
"Repo-specific risks / edge-cases" subsection** — quirks unique to this repo
that interact with the change, or "None observed".

* **Auto-implement** small, unambiguous updates (new env var → README,
  workflow `paths` filter → new directory, npm script → command list,
  Node-version bump consistency) and call it out in the summary.
* **Prompt first** for anything ambiguous or structurally significant —
  rewriting a README section, adding a top-level doc, restructuring a workflow.

If nothing is warranted, say "no doc/workflow updates needed". Skip entirely
for pure Q&A turns.
